.TH zview "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zview \- window server and desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzview\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zview -- window server and desktop
Usage:	zview &
	Started at boot by /etc/rc when /etc/wgui exists.
	Hold the right button for the menu; Quit restores the
	text console.
.HE
.B zview
is the window server and desktop of the
.SM COHERENT
GUI on the hi-res (bitmap) display.
It loads the display driver
.B /drv/hr
and the pseudo-terminal driver
.BR /drv/pty ,
takes over the screen and keyboard, loads the fonts, reads the
application catalog, puts an icon on the desktop for each catalog
entry, and runs the desktop start-up script.
It takes no arguments.
At boot,
.B /etc/rc
starts it when the file
.B /etc/wgui
exists \- create that file to enable the GUI; it can equally be
started by hand from the console:
.BR "zview &" .
.PP
The desktop shows icons at the top left: one placeholder per catalog
application (no process exists behind it \- clicking it launches the
program) and one icon per hidden window (marked with a corner badge;
clicking it restores the window).
Every window has a 3-button mouse and follows the same conventions:
.IP \fBmenus\fR
pop up under the
.B right
button and work by press-and-hold: hold the button down, drag onto an
entry \- it highlights as the pointer crosses it \- and release to
choose it.
Releasing outside the menu, or without moving onto an entry, chooses
nothing.
The keyboard is ignored while a menu is up.
Pressing on the empty desktop opens the desktop menu; pressing on a
window opens that window's menu.
.IP "\fBdesktop menu\fR"
lists the catalog applications (a single-instance entry raises its
existing window instead of starting a second copy), then
.BR "Switch to..." ,
a dialog of all open windows, and
.BR Quit ,
which confirms and then shuts the GUI down, restoring the text
console.
.IP "\fBwindow menu\fR"
starts with the entries the application asked for (from
.BR New ,
.BR Open ,
.BR Save ,
.BR Cut ,
.BR Copy ,
.BR Paste ,
.BR Settings ,
.BR Help ),
then
.BR Move ,
.B Stretch
(resizable windows only),
.BR Front ,
.BR Back ,
.B Hide
and
.BR Quit .
Some applications confirm Quit first.
.IP \fBraising\fR
a left click anywhere on a window brings it to the front and gives it
the keyboard;
.B Front
and
.B Back
restack it from the menu.
Every key goes to the focused (most recently raised) window \- there
are no desktop keyboard shortcuts.
.IP \fBmoving\fR
drag the title bar directly with the left button.
Alternatively pick
.B Move
from the window menu: press the left button to grab the window, drag
the outline where it should go, and release to place it \- a right or
middle press cancels instead.
.IP \fBresizing\fR
pick
.B Stretch
from the window menu (resizable windows only), grab with the left
button, and drag the window's bottom-right corner; on release the
application is told its new size.
Windows are clamped to the screen and to a 48x48 minimum.
.IP \fBhiding\fR
.B Hide
takes the window off the screen and leaves a desktop icon; clicking
the icon puts the window back exactly where it was.
.SH "COPY AND PASTE"
Text moves between windows in two independent ways.
.PP
The quick way is the
.IR selection :
sweep over text with the
.B left
button held down \- it highlights \- and release; the swept text
becomes the PRIMARY selection.
A
.B middle
click then pastes it into the window
.I "under the pointer"
at the click position, deliberately
.I without
raising that window, so text can be carried into a partly covered
window without disturbing the stacking.
Only one window at a time shows a selection highlight; making a new
selection clears the old one, but the stored text remains until
replaced, so it can be pasted repeatedly.
This works in both directions between any windows that support
selection \- sweep in a terminal and middle-click into the editor, or
sweep in the editor (or the manual browser) and middle-click into a
terminal.
.PP
The durable way is the
.IR clipboard ,
driven by the
.BR Cut ,
.B Copy
and
.B Paste
entries an application puts in its window menu (or their function
keys, where it has them).
It is a separate store: it survives further selections and is
explicit \- nothing reaches the clipboard until a Cut or Copy.
In a terminal window,
.B Copy
puts the current selection on the clipboard and
.B Paste
types the clipboard to the shell; in the editor, Cut/Copy/Paste act
on the selected text.
The two stores can hold different text at the same time.
.SH OPTIONS
.B zview
itself takes no options, but every GUI application accepts these,
consumed before its own arguments:
.DS
.ta 6n 20n
	\fB\-T \fItitle\fR	window title
	\fB\-I \fIfile\fB.icn\fR	icon (under /usr/hr/icons) when hidden
	\fB\-S \fIW\fBx\fIH\fR	content size in pixels (resizable
		applications only)
	\fB\-P \fIX\fB,\fIY\fR	window position in screen pixels
	\fB\-H\fR	start hidden, as a desktop icon
.DE
The one global key chord lives in the display driver:
.B ALT+CTRL+HELP
forcibly unloads it and shuts the desktop down \- the emergency exit
for a hung screen.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	/etc/wgui	if present, rc starts the GUI
	/usr/hr/etc/apps	application catalog
	/usr/hr/etc/rc	desktop start-up script
	/usr/hr/icons/*.icn	icons
	/usr/hr/fonts/*.hf	fonts
	/drv/hr, /drv/pty	drivers, loaded at start-up
	/wscrash	crash status of a dead server
.DE
Each
.B apps
line reads
\fIname\fB:\fIpath\fB:\fImulti\fB:\fIicon\fB:\fIX\fB,\fIY\fR
\- menu label, program, whether many copies may run (1) or one (0),
the placeholder icon, and where a launched window goes; the last two
fields are optional and
.B #
starts a comment.
The
.B rc
script runs at desktop start-up for anything that should be resident
from the beginning; each command must end in
.BR & .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zterm ,
.BR zedit ,
.BR zfile ,
.BR zmail ,
.BR zman ,
.BR zcalc ,
.BR zprint ,
.BR zmon ,
.BR zclock ,
.B zpuzzle
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`zview: no hi-res display on this machine';
`zview: a window server is already running' (if the driver was left
loaded, `/etc/uload /drv/hr' recovers).
If the server dies, a watchdog writes the wait status to
.BR /wscrash ,
restores the text console, and reports
`zview: server died -- screen and keyboard restored'.
.SH NOTES
At most 16 windows can be open and the catalog holds at most 12
applications.
The screen is 1024x800 pixels; windows are clamped to it.
Applications are best launched from icons, the desktop menu or the
.B apps
catalog rather than made resident in
.BR rc :
a placeholder icon costs no memory until it is clicked.
